Upon investigating an issue with one of the applications on the Kubernetes cluster, the team discovered that the service was configured with an incorrect node port. 
We need to update the service as follows:

Update service-t5q3 service to use node port 30099.

Service 'service-t5q3' nodePort is '30099'

Website is up and accessible

SOLUTION
To update an existing Service's nodePort, you can't simply change it in place with kubectl patch on all Kubernetes versions (and even when it works, the old port may linger in some setups). 
The most reliable approach is to patch it, but if that fails, delete and recreate. Here are both methods.

First: Check the current Service
bash
kubectl get svc service-t5q3 -o yaml
Note the existing ports block (port, targetPort, protocol) so you preserve them. NodePorts must be in the range 30000–32767 (default), and 30099 is valid.

Option 1: Patch the Service (try this first)
bash
kubectl patch svc service-t5q3 -p '{"spec":{"ports":[{"port":80,"targetPort":80,"protocol":"TCP","nodePort":30099}]}}'
Important: Replace port and targetPort with the values currently configured on service-t5q3 (from the kubectl get svc -o yaml output above). 
If you patch with wrong port/targetPort values, you'll break routing to the Pods. If your Service has multiple ports, you must include all of them in the patch.

Option 2: Delete and Recreate (most reliable)
If patching errors out (some versions reject nodePort changes), recreate the Service:

bash
# Save current definition first
kubectl get svc service-t5q3 -o yaml > service-t5q3-backup.yaml

# Delete it
kubectl delete svc service-t5q3
Then apply the corrected manifest (adjust selector, port, and targetPort to match your app):

yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: service-t5q3
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: <your-app-label>      # Must match the Pods' labels
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30099
      protocol: TCP
bash
kubectl apply -f service-t5q3.yaml
Verification
bash
# Confirm nodePort is 30099 and endpoints are populated
kubectl describe svc service-t5q3
Check that:

Type: is NodePort

NodePort: shows 30099

Endpoints: lists Pod IPs (not <none> — if empty, your selector is wrong)

Then test access:

bash
kubectl get nodes -o wide          # get node IP
curl http://<NODE-IP>:30099
For local clusters (minikube/kind), use minikube service service-t5q3 or a port-forward if node IPs aren't directly reachable.

Quick troubleshooting: If the website isn't up after the change, the most common causes are (1) selector not matching Pod labels, (2) targetPort not matching the container's listening port, or (3) the old Service wasn't fully deleted before recreation — re-run kubectl describe svc service-t5q3 and check Endpoints.
